## 4. Durable behavior requirements

### General decision-making / teamplay
- Only the dedicated cover/goalie role should be passive; other teammates should remain aggressive within role selection.
- A transition player should wait about 0.17–0.20 s after the first teammate shot before acting (not the old ~0.10 s).
- Transition bump/demolition target: when transition player is out of defense, a teammate has possession/shot, and enemy defenders block the route, target the closest relevant defender; maximum two defenders.
- Team statuses used include `NO_SHOT`, `SHOT_FOUND`, `ANY_SHOT_FOUND`, `LOW_BOOST`, `DEFEND`, and `SHOT_ABANDONED`. A real teammate `SHOT_FOUND` should take precedence over `ANY_SHOT_FOUND`.
- Defense should not use wall-dash behavior (`allow_wall_dash=False`).
- Emergency clear/save logic matters in deep own-half situations; do not weaken it while changing attack routines.
- Goalie must not rotate unnecessarily in goal to face the ball when the ball is travelling toward the own goal. Prioritize protecting the net over needless aerial/boost detours.

### Shots and mechanics
- Shot planner horizon should generally consider 1–3 s opportunities, not only ~0.45 s.
- Low boost (below roughly 12) should discourage complex shot types where possible.
- Jump-shot shot vector should point toward the opponent goal; tilt-back then flip/dodge. Very quick shots may skip the tilt-back and use a forward flip.
- Ground-shot should use the jump-shot shot vector/contact target logic where relevant and refresh stale contact data.
- Aerial should aim the car's hitbox/nose at a deliberate ball contact point and predict the resulting shot direction toward goal. Avoid aiming at the ball centre blindly.
- Aerial should be boost-aware and should not spend boost wastefully gaining all its momentum after takeoff if sufficient runway exists before takeoff.
- Aerial orientation should be time-aware: use enough alignment for the available time, but do not over-rotate or miss a fast contact trying to achieve perfect orientation.
- Side shots should be used only when the geometry and field position make sense, especially in the attacking half. Avoid weak side-flips in the own half.
- For directional jump-shot dodge decisions, compare ball position to the interception point, not merely the car.
- User dislikes backflip-heavy dodge behavior and excessive setup that loses good immediate shots.

## 5. Wall behavior — live implementation

- The active branch currently contains a standalone `wall_approach` routine, selected by `tools.find_wall_drive()` and committed through the agent's shot-selection path. Do not assume the older removal notes below describe the current live branch.
- The wall-drive routine is limited to side walls. It must not turn the own back wall into an attacking route or pull the goalie away from the goal line.
- Wall-drive selection must require the predicted ball centre to be physically close to the side-wall collision plane. A ball merely travelling toward a wall is not a valid wall-drive intercept.
- Car reachability must include the ground approach, rounded floor-to-wall transition, wall climb, current heading/velocity, acceleration, boost budget, and a safety margin. A straight-line distance or fixed climb-speed estimate is not sufficient.
- The routine must distinguish ground approach, curved transition, stable wall attachment, and free flight after losing the wall. Do not continue wall steering while the car is falling away from the wall; hand off to recovery and let normal shot selection reconsider after landing.
- Party Cannon's `Drive.cs`, `Car.cs`, and `Field.cs` are the reference for surface-aware distance, turn-radius, landing prediction, and smooth surface transitions. Volt's current arena model is still a side-wall approximation, not a full mesh simulation.
- Preserve ordinary ground/jump/aerial shot geometry and defensive behaviour when no physically reachable wall intercept exists.

## 6. Current Aerial implementation notes

- The live `Aerial` class in `routines of volt` uses Party Cannon-inspired shot direction prediction and hitbox standoff targeting.
- It predicts the ball at `intercept_time`, derives `self.shot_vector`, applies `ShotSurfaceGeometry.adjust_aerial`, then computes a hitbox-aware `self.target`.
- Its current launch condition is in the `if self.jumping or not agent.me.airborne:` block. On the first grounded tick it sets `self.jumping = True` and starts the jump immediately; this is why it currently launches from the floor without a distinct ground acceleration phase.
- Current jump simulation adds first jump impulse/hold acceleration and optional second jump impulse; fast aerial holds the first jump then pulses the second jump. It then orients toward the predicted hitbox target and uses Party Cannon-style boost estimation / forward-projection boost control.
- The current live branch does not contain the earlier proposed ground-speed build phase. The first grounded tick enters the existing jump sequence immediately. Do not assume the old 0.38 s ground setup, 1150 forward-speed threshold, or 0.88 alignment threshold are present unless they are re-added and verified in the live file.

